1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,570
Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,570 --> 00:00:22,802
Tonight we are going to spend one gentle evening in Athens, about two and a half thousand years ago, when the great marble temple on the Acropolis was still new.

3
00:00:22,802 --> 00:00:27,512
There will be no battles tonight, and no speeches that matter.

4
00:00:27,512 --> 00:00:33,666
Only lamps being lit, supper being shared, and a city settling slowly into sleep.

5
00:00:35,366 --> 00:00:37,572
Let yourself get comfortable.

6
00:00:37,572 --> 00:00:43,504
Let the pillow take the weight of your head, and let your jaw loosen a little.

7
00:00:43,504 --> 00:00:50,425
Breathe in slowly, as if you could smell warm stone and wild thyme after a long summer day.

8
00:00:50,425 --> 00:00:57,574
Then breathe out, longer than you breathed in, and feel the day begin to slip away behind you.

9
00:00:59,274 --> 00:01:02,313
We arrive just as the sun goes down.

10
00:01:02,313 --> 00:01:11,431
A high rock rises above the rooftops, and along its top runs a row of pale columns, catching the last light.

11
00:01:11,431 --> 00:01:25,024
Below it, the houses of the city crowd together, roofed in red clay tiles, and here and there a doorway already glows with the small yellow flame of an oil lamp.

12
00:01:26,723 --> 00:01:31,810
You do not need to remember any names tonight, or follow any story.

13
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:43,503
We will simply wander, from the hillside to the market, into a family's courtyard, past a quiet dinner among friends, and up to the temple under the moon.

14
00:01:43,503 --> 00:01:49,273
If you drift away somewhere along the path, that is exactly as it should be.

15
00:01:58,973 --> 00:02:08,295
We are sitting on a low hill west of the Acropolis, on a broad shelf of grey rock worn smooth by thousands of feet.

16
00:02:08,295 --> 00:02:17,132
In the daytime, this is the Pnyx, where the citizens of Athens gather on assembly days to listen and to vote.

17
00:02:17,132 --> 00:02:25,725
This evening it is empty, except for us, a few tufts of dry grass, and the warm air rising from the stone.

18
00:02:27,425 --> 00:02:33,284
To the east, beyond the city, there is a long mountain called Hymettus.

19
00:02:33,284 --> 00:02:43,599
As the sun sinks behind us, its whole slope begins to change colour, from gold to rose, and from rose to a deep, soft violet.

20
00:02:43,599 --> 00:02:51,851
Poets called Athens the violet-crowned city, and on an evening like this one, it is easy to see why.

21
00:02:53,551 --> 00:02:57,091
Hymettus is famous for something else as well.

22
00:02:57,091 --> 00:03:08,249
On its slopes grow thyme and sage and savory, and the bees that visit them make a pale, fragrant honey that the Athenians prize above all others.

23
00:03:08,249 --> 00:03:19,791
Somewhere up there, in the dusk, rows of hives made of clay and woven straw are falling quiet, the last bees crawling home through their little doors.

24
00:03:21,491 --> 00:03:31,355
Just below the southern side of the Acropolis, you can make out a great hollow in the hillside, a curve of seats cut into the slope.

25
00:03:31,355 --> 00:03:34,343
This is the theatre of the god Dionysus.

26
00:03:34,343 --> 00:03:41,292
In the spring festival, thousands of Athenians sit there from dawn till dusk, watching plays.

27
00:03:41,292 --> 00:03:51,081
Tonight its seats are empty and silver grey, and a hare is loping slowly across the circle of beaten earth where the chorus dances.

28
00:03:52,781 --> 00:04:02,958
Below us, the city spreads out in every direction, a soft jumble of flat roofs and tiled roofs, narrow lanes and small courtyards.

29
00:04:02,958 --> 00:04:07,499
There are no tall towers here, apart from the rock itself.

30
00:04:07,499 --> 00:04:18,381
Most houses have only one floor, or two at the most, and their walls are made of sun-dried mud brick, plastered and washed in pale colours.

31
00:04:20,081 --> 00:04:30,568
Far off to the south west, where the land runs down towards the sea, two long walls stretch away side by side, straight as ruled lines.

32
00:04:30,568 --> 00:04:41,210
They run for miles, all the way down to the harbour town of Piraeus, so that the road between the city and its ships is always protected.

33
00:04:41,210 --> 00:04:45,638
Tonight they are just two faint grey threads in the dusk.

34
00:04:47,338 --> 00:04:56,392
At the end of them, the sea lies smooth and pale, and in the harbours a scatter of tiny lights is coming out, one by one.

35
00:04:56,392 --> 00:05:12,554
Those are the lamps of taverns along the waterfront, and of sailors sitting on the decks of merchant ships, and of the watchmen beside the long sheds where the warships rest, pulled up out of the water for the night.

36
00:05:14,254 --> 00:05:26,297
Closer at hand, a goatherd is bringing his small flock down the next slope, the bells around their necks clinking softly as they pick their way between the rocks.

37
00:05:26,297 --> 00:05:38,638
He calls to them now and then, a low, wordless sound, and they answer with a bleat or two, and then the clinking moves away along the path and fades into the evening.

38
00:05:40,338 --> 00:05:45,764
The first star appears over the shoulder of Hymettus, and then another.

39
00:05:45,764 --> 00:05:53,329
The rock beneath us still holds the warmth of the afternoon, like a stone taken from beside a fire.

40
00:05:53,329 --> 00:06:07,389
A little owl, small and round, with pale speckled feathers, lands on a boulder a few steps away, turns its head towards us, blinks its yellow eyes, and settles down to watch the night.

41
00:06:17,089 --> 00:06:26,858
We walk down from the hill on a path of beaten earth, between low walls where lizards have stopped sunning themselves for the day.

42
00:06:26,858 --> 00:06:34,824
The path joins a wider street, and the street opens into a broad, level space, edged with long colonnades.

43
00:06:34,824 --> 00:06:45,420
This is the Agora, the heart of Athenian life, where people come to buy and sell, to argue, to gossip, and to idle away an hour in the shade.

44
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:50,761
At this time of evening, the market is closing.

45
00:06:50,761 --> 00:07:07,807
The sellers of fish have long since packed up their baskets, and the last of the vegetable sellers are lifting their trestles, folding their striped awnings, and tying their unsold onions and cabbages into cloth bundles.

46
00:07:07,807 --> 00:07:14,704
A boy sweeps up the leaves and straw from the beaten floor with a broom of bundled twigs.

47
00:07:16,403 --> 00:07:22,795
Along the northern side runs a long, roofed colonnade with a solid wall at the back.

48
00:07:22,795 --> 00:07:34,361
Athenians call it the Painted Stoa, because inside, on great wooden panels, famous artists have painted scenes from the old stories and the city's past.

49
00:07:34,361 --> 00:07:44,329
In the half light, you can just make out the shapes of horses and ships, of warriors and gods, their colours softening into shadow.

50
00:07:46,029 --> 00:07:54,181
In the daytime, people stroll beneath these columns to keep out of the sun, or sit on the steps with a friend.

51
00:07:54,181 --> 00:08:01,963
Philosophers will one day gather here to teach, and their followers will be named after the porch itself.

52
00:08:01,963 --> 00:08:13,229
But tonight, only an old man is left, sitting with his back against a column, a cloak around his shoulders, quietly watching the swallows dart overhead.

53
00:08:14,929 --> 00:08:21,567
On the western side of the square stands a small round building with a cone shaped roof.

54
00:08:21,567 --> 00:08:23,000
This is the Tholos.

55
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:32,053
Inside it, the members of the city's council who are on duty this month eat their meals together, at the city's expense.

56
00:08:32,053 --> 00:08:41,105
And every night, some of them sleep here too, so that the city is never without someone to answer a message in the dark.

57
00:08:42,805 --> 00:08:51,601
Through the open door of the Tholos comes a soft glow, and the murmur of men talking over the remains of their supper.

58
00:08:51,601 --> 00:08:57,340
There is the smell of bread, and of cheese and olives, and of a little onion.

59
00:08:57,340 --> 00:08:59,800
Someone laughs quietly at a joke.

60
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:08,149
A servant comes out with a jug and walks across to the fountain house to fill it with fresh water for the night.

61
00:09:09,849 --> 00:09:19,339
At the corners of the streets, and outside many doorways, stand square stone pillars with a carved head on top, bearded and calm.

62
00:09:19,339 --> 00:09:26,254
These are herms, and they belong to the god Hermes, protector of travellers and of boundaries.

63
00:09:26,254 --> 00:09:30,301
As people pass them, some touch them lightly, for luck.

64
00:09:30,301 --> 00:09:36,260
One has a small wreath of leaves hung around its neck, already beginning to wilt.

65
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:48,835
High in the plane trees along the edge of the square, the cicadas, which have sung all through the hot afternoon, are finally slowing down.

66
00:09:48,835 --> 00:09:58,537
One stops, and then another, until only a single cicada is still rasping away, slower and slower, like a clock running down.

67
00:09:58,537 --> 00:10:04,328
And then it, too, falls silent, and the evening becomes very quiet indeed.

68
00:10:06,028 --> 00:10:15,504
Near the lawcourts, on a small stone stand, there is a water clock, a clay jar with a little bronze spout near the bottom.

69
00:10:15,504 --> 00:10:25,136
In the daytime it is used to time the speeches in court, each speaker allowed only as long as it takes the water to run out.

70
00:10:25,136 --> 00:10:31,350
Tonight it stands empty and dry, and no one is in a hurry to be anywhere at all.

71
00:10:33,050 --> 00:10:44,284
A slave from one of the public offices walks slowly along the colonnade with a taper, lighting the lamps that hang on chains between the columns.

72
00:10:44,284 --> 00:10:50,404
Each small clay lamp holds a pool of olive oil and a twist of linen for a wick.

73
00:10:50,404 --> 00:10:59,546
One by one, the flames come up, small and steady, and the long row of columns glows softly against the deepening blue.

74
00:11:09,246 --> 00:11:18,682
Let us follow a narrow lane away from the square, uphill a little, towards the houses that cluster under the rocky slopes.

75
00:11:18,682 --> 00:11:22,936
The lane is just wide enough for a donkey with baskets.

76
00:11:22,936 --> 00:11:33,609
Its walls are blank and plain, broken only by wooden doors, and above them, now and then, a small high window shows a square of lamplight.

77
00:11:35,309 --> 00:11:42,061
We stop at one door, painted a faded blue, with a little stone herm standing beside it.

78
00:11:42,061 --> 00:11:49,821
The door is fastened with a wooden bar on the inside, but it has been left open for the evening air.

79
00:11:49,821 --> 00:12:00,375
A worn stone sill lies across the threshold, hollowed in the middle by many years of feet, and a scent of cooking drifts out to meet us.

80
00:12:02,076 --> 00:12:07,200
Inside, a short passage leads into a courtyard, open to the sky.

81
00:12:07,200 --> 00:12:10,323
This is the centre of an Athenian home.

82
00:12:10,323 --> 00:12:26,417
All the rooms of the house open onto it, the storerooms and the kitchen, the bedrooms and the room for guests, so that the family lives looking inward, around this small square of beaten earth and sky.

83
00:12:28,117 --> 00:12:39,330
In the middle of the courtyard stands a small stone altar, about knee high, dedicated to Zeus of the Courtyard, the guardian of the household.

84
00:12:39,330 --> 00:12:53,781
A few grains of incense smoulder on top, sending up a thin grey thread of sweet smoke that rises straight and slow into the still air, and then spreads and vanishes above the rooftop.

85
00:12:55,481 --> 00:13:02,622
In one corner there is a well, with a round stone mouth and a rope hanging from a wooden beam.

86
00:13:02,622 --> 00:13:11,815
Beside it stands a tall clay water jar, with three handles, two at the sides for lifting and one at the back for pouring.

87
00:13:11,815 --> 00:13:19,716
Its surface is cool and slightly damp to the touch, and a little frog sits at its foot, perfectly still.

88
00:13:21,416 --> 00:13:29,316
Along one side of the courtyard runs a narrow porch, with a few wooden posts holding up a tiled roof.

89
00:13:29,316 --> 00:13:46,290
Beneath it stands an upright loom, almost as tall as a person, with its threads hanging down in long, even rows, weighted at the bottom by little clay pyramids that sway and click softly whenever the cloth is touched.

90
00:13:47,990 --> 00:14:00,562
The mistress of the house has been weaving here through the afternoon, a length of fine wool cloth in a soft madder red, with a narrow band of pattern along the edge.

91
00:14:00,562 --> 00:14:03,970
Now she has put down her shuttle for the day.

92
00:14:03,970 --> 00:14:12,225
The half finished cloth hangs quiet on the loom, and her basket of wool sits beside it, spun into neat balls.

93
00:14:13,925 --> 00:14:22,583
Above the storerooms, a wooden ladder leads up to a second floor, where the family sleep in two small rooms under the roof.

94
00:14:22,583 --> 00:14:30,960
Their windows are small and high, with wooden shutters, and one has been propped open to let in the cooler evening air.

95
00:14:30,960 --> 00:14:39,056
From up there, if you stood on tiptoe, you could just see the columns of the temple over the neighbouring rooftops.

96
00:14:40,755 --> 00:14:48,557
A fig tree grows against the far wall, its broad, rough leaves spreading over the corner like a canopy.

97
00:14:48,557 --> 00:14:55,980
A few ripe figs hang among them, purple and soft, and one has fallen and split open on the ground.

98
00:14:55,980 --> 00:15:05,524
The tree has been there longer than anyone in the house can remember, and its roots have lifted the edge of the paving stones.

99
00:15:07,223 --> 00:15:17,151
Against the walls of the storeroom stand tall clay jars, some as high as your waist, for olive oil, wine, barley and dried figs.

100
00:15:17,151 --> 00:15:22,657
Their mouths are sealed with plugs of clay or covered with flat stones.

101
00:15:22,657 --> 00:15:32,119
In the warm air, the whole house smells faintly of oil and earth, of wool and smoke, and of the figs ripening on the tree.

102
00:15:41,819 --> 00:15:52,412
In the kitchen, a small room at the corner of the courtyard, a cooking pot sits over a portable clay brazier filled with glowing charcoal.

103
00:15:52,412 --> 00:15:56,480
The smoke drifts out through a gap in the roof tiles.

104
00:15:56,480 --> 00:16:05,692
An older woman of the household stirs the pot with a wooden spoon, tastes it, adds a pinch of salt, and nods to herself.

105
00:16:07,391 --> 00:16:11,371
Supper tonight is simple, as most Athenian meals are.

106
00:16:11,371 --> 00:16:20,607
There are flat cakes of barley, kneaded with water and a little oil, and a few loaves of wheat bread bought from the baker.

107
00:16:20,607 --> 00:16:24,437
There are olives, black and green, in a small bowl.

108
00:16:24,437 --> 00:16:32,095
There is a soft white cheese of goat's milk, and a dish of lentils cooked slowly with onion and thyme.

109
00:16:33,795 --> 00:16:43,345
There is a small fish too, grilled over the charcoal and laid on a dish with a drizzle of oil and a squeeze of something sharp.

110
00:16:43,345 --> 00:16:54,248
The Athenians love fish, and the market has plenty of it, sardines and anchovies and little red mullet brought up from the harbour every morning.

111
00:16:54,248 --> 00:16:59,737
Meat is for festival days, when there is a sacrifice and everyone shares.

112
00:17:01,437 --> 00:17:09,643
The family eat together on stools and benches under the porch, with the lamp set on a little table between them.

113
00:17:09,643 --> 00:17:15,505
The father talks about the price of olive oil, and about a neighbour's new mule.

114
00:17:15,505 --> 00:17:24,370
The children are hungry and eat quickly, dipping their bread in the oil, and their mother tells them gently to slow down.

115
00:17:26,070 --> 00:17:36,381
Afterwards, there are figs from the tree, and a few almonds, and a small piece of honeycomb from Hymettus, dripping golden onto a plate.

116
00:17:36,381 --> 00:17:40,551
The children share it carefully, licking their fingers.

117
00:17:40,551 --> 00:17:51,393
The grandmother takes only a little, and closes her eyes as she tastes it, the way people do when a flavour reminds them of something long ago.

118
00:17:53,093 --> 00:18:01,137
The lamp they eat by is a small dish of red clay, shaped like a flattened bowl with a spout at one end.

119
00:18:01,137 --> 00:18:05,197
It holds olive oil, and the wick rests in the spout.

120
00:18:05,197 --> 00:18:13,163
Its flame is no bigger than a fingertip, and it gives a soft, warm light that does not reach very far.

121
00:18:13,163 --> 00:18:19,098
The corners of the courtyard sink into shadow, and the sky above grows deep.

122
00:18:20,798 --> 00:18:28,304
When the meal is done, the children are sent to wash their hands and faces in a basin by the well.

123
00:18:28,304 --> 00:18:31,597
The water is cold, and they gasp and laugh.

124
00:18:31,597 --> 00:18:43,774
Then the grandmother sits down on a low stool beneath the fig tree, and the youngest climbs into her lap, and she begins to tell a story, in a slow, low voice.

125
00:18:45,474 --> 00:18:49,973
It is one of the old tales that every Athenian child knows.

126
00:18:49,973 --> 00:18:57,902
The story goes that long ago, two gods wished to be the patron of this city, and each offered it a gift.

127
00:18:57,902 --> 00:19:02,553
One struck the rock and brought forth a spring of salt water.

128
00:19:02,553 --> 00:19:05,526
The other planted the first olive tree.

129
00:19:05,526 --> 00:19:11,778
And the people chose the olive, and so the city has belonged to Athena ever since.

130
00:19:13,478 --> 00:19:19,991
The child is already half asleep, her head heavy against her grandmother's shoulder.

131
00:19:19,991 --> 00:19:25,652
The story slows, and pauses, and goes on a little more, and pauses again.

132
00:19:25,652 --> 00:19:31,002
Above them, a bat flickers across the open square of sky and is gone.

133
00:19:31,002 --> 00:19:37,670
The grandmother hums softly, an old tune without words, and rocks gently on her stool.

134
00:19:47,370 --> 00:19:54,596
On the other side of the lane, in a neighbour's house, a small dinner among friends is going on.

135
00:19:54,596 --> 00:19:59,639
The room where it takes place is called the andron, the men's room.

136
00:19:59,639 --> 00:20:12,736
It is square, with a raised edge of plaster running all around the walls, and on that raised edge stand wooden couches, seven of them, each with a mattress and soft cushions.

137
00:20:14,435 --> 00:20:28,174
The guests have washed their hands and taken off their sandals at the door, and now they recline on the couches, leaning on their left elbows, their cloaks loosely draped around them.

138
00:20:28,174 --> 00:20:39,886
In front of each couch stands a small low table, and on the tables are the remains of the meal, crusts of bread, a few olive stones, a scatter of nutshells.

139
00:20:41,586 --> 00:20:55,191
At the start of the drinking, before anything else, the host poured a few drops of unmixed wine onto the floor, as an offering to the gods, and everyone sang a short hymn together.

140
00:20:55,191 --> 00:21:06,226
Now each guest wears a garland on his head, woven of myrtle and ivy, and the leaves give off a fresh green scent that mingles with the lamp smoke.

141
00:21:07,926 --> 00:21:14,816
In the middle of the room stands a large bowl of painted clay on a three-legged stand.

142
00:21:14,816 --> 00:21:17,700
This is the krater, the mixing bowl.

143
00:21:17,700 --> 00:21:27,955
In it, wine is mixed with water, usually far more water than wine, for to drink wine unmixed is thought rude and rather foolish.

144
00:21:27,955 --> 00:21:34,123
A boy with a jug moves quietly from couch to couch, filling the shallow cups.

145
00:21:35,823 --> 00:21:41,023
The cups are wide and flat, on slender stems, painted black and red.

146
00:21:41,023 --> 00:21:51,115
When one is tipped back to drink, a little picture appears at the bottom, a dolphin perhaps, or a dancing figure, or a running hare.

147
00:21:51,115 --> 00:21:58,607
One of the guests smiles at the hare in the bottom of his cup, and turns it to show his neighbour.

148
00:22:00,307 --> 00:22:03,161
Tonight the gathering is a gentle one.

149
00:22:03,161 --> 00:22:15,102
The host has chosen a sensible man to decide how strong the wine will be, and how many bowls will be mixed, and he has decided on only a few, and well watered.

150
00:22:15,102 --> 00:22:26,142
The talk drifts easily, from a play that some of them saw at the theatre in the spring, to the vines on a cousin's farm, to the meaning of a dream.

151
00:22:27,842 --> 00:22:31,015
After a while, someone asks for the lyre.

152
00:22:31,015 --> 00:22:39,139
It is passed from hand to hand until it reaches a guest who can play, a quiet man with grey in his beard.

153
00:22:39,139 --> 00:22:53,143
He tunes the strings slowly, one by one, then begins a simple song, and the others listen with their eyes half closed, and one or two of them join in on the last line of each verse.

154
00:22:54,843 --> 00:23:03,464
Along the walls, the light comes from lamps set on tall bronze stands, each with a little dish of oil at the top.

155
00:23:03,464 --> 00:23:07,584
The flames lean and straighten whenever someone moves.

156
00:23:07,584 --> 00:23:20,401
Painted on the plaster, just below the ceiling, runs a band of red and black, a pattern of interlocking lines and leaves that seems to wander on and on around the room.

157
00:23:22,101 --> 00:23:24,412
Later, the talk grows slower.

158
00:23:24,412 --> 00:23:32,937
Someone sets a riddle, and the others think about it for a long time and cannot solve it, and nobody minds.

159
00:23:32,937 --> 00:23:47,359
One guest has quietly fallen asleep on his couch, his cup still resting in his hand, and the boy with the jug gently takes it from him and sets it down on the table without a sound.

160
00:23:49,059 --> 00:23:58,330
At last the host rises and thanks his friends, and they begin to leave, one by one, putting on their sandals at the door.

161
00:23:58,330 --> 00:24:07,600
Outside, each is met by a servant with a small torch, or a lantern of horn, to light the way home through the dark lanes.

162
00:24:07,600 --> 00:24:15,107
Their footsteps and low voices wander off in different directions, and the lane falls quiet again.

163
00:24:16,807 --> 00:24:25,440
In the empty room, the boy blows out the lamps on their tall stands, one after another, until only one is left.

164
00:24:25,440 --> 00:24:32,362
He gathers the cups and stacks them in a basket, and covers the mixing bowl with a cloth.

165
00:24:32,362 --> 00:24:42,706
Then he too lies down, on a mat in the corner of the courtyard, and pulls his cloak over his shoulders, and is asleep almost at once.

166
00:24:52,406 --> 00:24:58,256
Now let us leave the houses behind and climb up towards the rock itself.

167
00:24:58,256 --> 00:25:07,355
The way winds up the western slope in long, easy turns, paved in places, worn smooth by the feet of processions.

168
00:25:07,355 --> 00:25:17,835
The moon has risen now, almost full, and the path is pale and clear in front of us, and our shadows walk beside us on the stones.

169
00:25:19,535 --> 00:25:26,513
As we climb, the city falls away below us, and the air grows a little cooler and fresher.

170
00:25:26,513 --> 00:25:40,548
We pass a small spring in a cave at the foot of the rock, where water drips steadily into a stone basin, and a few clay cups have been left on the ledge for anyone who is thirsty.

171
00:25:40,548 --> 00:25:44,154
The water is cold and tastes faintly of stone.

172
00:25:45,854 --> 00:25:54,479
At the top of the path, we come to the great gateway, a broad stairway of marble leading up between rows of columns.

173
00:25:54,479 --> 00:26:01,988
By day, people pass through here in a steady stream, carrying offerings, leading animals, chattering.

174
00:26:01,988 --> 00:26:10,985
Tonight there is only a single guard, sitting on a step with his spear leaning beside him, who nods to us without a word.

175
00:26:12,685 --> 00:26:22,981
Beyond the gateway, the top of the rock opens out like a wide, uneven plateau, crowded with shrines and statues and little altars.

176
00:26:22,981 --> 00:26:37,474
And there, ahead of us and to the right, stands the temple of Athena, the Parthenon, larger than anything else in the city, its rows of columns rising pale and still in the moonlight.

177
00:26:39,174 --> 00:26:51,193
The marble came from a mountain called Pentelikon, a few hours' walk to the north east, and it was dragged here block by block on heavy sledges and ox carts.

178
00:26:51,193 --> 00:26:59,461
When it is new, as it is now, it is almost white, with the faintest warm tint, like honey stirred into milk.

179
00:26:59,461 --> 00:27:02,982
In the moonlight it seems to glow from within.

180
00:27:04,682 --> 00:27:16,419
Up close, the columns are enormous, each made of many drums of stone stacked one upon another, so smoothly fitted that the joins are almost invisible.

181
00:27:16,419 --> 00:27:18,610
They are not quite straight.

182
00:27:18,610 --> 00:27:30,581
Each one swells very slightly in the middle, and leans very slightly inwards, so gently that the eye cannot see it, and yet the whole temple looks alive.

183
00:27:32,281 --> 00:27:52,400
High above the columns, around the outside of the building, runs a band of carved figures, and inside the colonnade there is another, long and narrow, showing a great procession, riders and walkers, musicians and elders, young women carrying bowls, all moving the same way.

184
00:27:52,400 --> 00:27:55,716
In daylight they are painted in soft colours.

185
00:27:55,716 --> 00:28:00,654
In the moonlight, they are only shapes, walking quietly on forever.

186
00:28:02,354 --> 00:28:13,770
Inside the temple, in the dark, stands a great statue of Athena, many times the height of a person, made of ivory and gold on a frame of wood.

187
00:28:13,770 --> 00:28:18,111
Her face and arms are ivory, her robe and armour gold.

188
00:28:18,111 --> 00:28:20,844
No lamp is lit before her tonight.

189
00:28:20,844 --> 00:28:28,722
The great doors are closed, and the goddess stands alone in the darkness, keeping her own silence.

190
00:28:30,422 --> 00:28:42,742
Out in the open, between the temple and the gateway, stands another Athena, made of bronze, so tall that her helmet and the tip of her spear rise above the rooftops.

191
00:28:42,742 --> 00:28:53,867
It is said that sailors coming home round the southern cape can see the glint of that spear point from far out at sea, and know they are nearly home.

192
00:28:55,567 --> 00:29:02,263
From the northern edge of the rock, the whole plain of Attica lies open beneath the moon.

193
00:29:02,263 --> 00:29:12,194
There are dark hills on three sides, and olive groves in long grey rows, and the white threads of roads leading out to the villages.

194
00:29:12,194 --> 00:29:21,146
And to the south west, beyond the Long Walls, the sea shines like a sheet of beaten silver, all the way to the islands.

195
00:29:22,846 --> 00:29:31,049
Not far away, inside a little walled enclosure, grows an olive tree, old and gnarled and silver leaved.

196
00:29:31,049 --> 00:29:37,659
People say it is the very tree that Athena planted, the gift that won her the city.

197
00:29:37,659 --> 00:29:46,420
Its leaves stir faintly in the night air, showing their pale undersides to the moon, and then turn dark again.

198
00:29:48,119 --> 00:29:57,064
On a ledge high on the temple, half hidden in the shadow of the carved stone, a pair of little owls have made their home.

199
00:29:57,064 --> 00:30:02,016
They are Athena's birds, and nobody would dream of disturbing them.

200
00:30:02,016 --> 00:30:13,399
Every now and then one of them calls, a soft, rising whistle, and the other answers from somewhere above, and the sound drifts out over the sleeping city.

201
00:30:23,099 --> 00:30:27,709
From the edge of the rock, we look down over Athens by night.

202
00:30:27,709 --> 00:30:30,429
Most of the lamps have gone out now.

203
00:30:30,429 --> 00:30:42,595
Here and there, a single doorway still glows, where someone is sitting up late, or a baby is being fed, or a baker has already started the dough for the morning.

204
00:30:42,595 --> 00:30:47,355
Otherwise, the city is a soft grey sea of roofs under the moon.

205
00:30:49,055 --> 00:30:53,046
Down in the Agora, the Tholos is still faintly lit.

206
00:30:53,046 --> 00:31:02,279
Inside, the councillors on duty for the night are lying on simple beds along the curved wall, wrapped in their cloaks.

207
00:31:02,279 --> 00:31:08,147
One of them is awake, sitting by the lamp, in case a messenger should come.

208
00:31:08,147 --> 00:31:10,886
But no messenger is coming tonight.

209
00:31:10,886 --> 00:31:14,485
Everything is quiet, and he lets his head nod.

210
00:31:16,185 --> 00:31:26,051
Along the walls of the city, the watchmen walk their slow rounds, from tower to tower, their sandals scuffing softly on the stone.

211
00:31:26,051 --> 00:31:35,311
They pause at the corners to look out over the dark fields and olive groves beyond, where nothing moves except the leaves.

212
00:31:35,311 --> 00:31:40,548
Then they turn and walk back again, and the night goes on, unhurried.

213
00:31:42,247 --> 00:31:50,414
In a little potter's workshop near the city gate, the kiln that was fired this afternoon is slowly cooling.

214
00:31:50,414 --> 00:31:56,977
Inside it, stacked carefully, are new cups and jugs and lamps, still too hot to touch.

215
00:31:56,977 --> 00:32:07,357
The potter has gone home to his supper, and the workshop is dark, except for a faint red glow through the cracks around the kiln's door.

216
00:32:09,057 --> 00:32:13,435
Far away, down at the harbour, the ships are resting too.

217
00:32:13,435 --> 00:32:23,267
The great warships lie in their long roofed sheds, drawn up on stone slipways, their oars stacked, their hulls black with pitch.

218
00:32:23,267 --> 00:32:33,483
The merchant ships sit at anchor in the bay, rocking gently, lanterns at their sterns, their crews asleep on deck under the warm sky.

219
00:32:35,183 --> 00:32:42,235
Overhead, the stars are thick and bright, and the Athenians know many of them by name and story.

220
00:32:42,235 --> 00:32:48,698
There is the hunter Orion, not yet risen, who will climb over the mountains before dawn.

221
00:32:48,698 --> 00:33:03,535
There is the little cluster of the Pleiades, the seven sisters, whose rising in early summer tells farmers it is time to harvest, and whose setting in the autumn warns sailors to bring their ships home.

222
00:33:05,235 --> 00:33:13,786
In the houses below, the families are sleeping on low beds with wooden frames and mattresses stuffed with wool.

223
00:33:13,786 --> 00:33:23,724
On the hottest nights, some carry their bedding up onto the flat roofs and sleep beneath the open sky, with only a light blanket.

224
00:33:23,724 --> 00:33:30,579
The grandmother from the courtyard is asleep at last, and the child is asleep beside her.

225
00:33:32,279 --> 00:33:41,430
Out on the hillsides, beyond the city walls, the olive groves stand in long silver rows, their leaves just stirring.

226
00:33:41,430 --> 00:33:48,530
Somewhere among them, a dog barks once, and then thinks better of it, and lies down again.

227
00:33:48,530 --> 00:33:56,813
A breeze comes up from the sea, very faint, carrying the smell of salt and pine resin and warm dry earth.

228
00:33:58,513 --> 00:34:09,115
In a stable at the edge of town, a donkey shifts its weight from one foot to the other and sighs, a long, deep sigh, the way donkeys do.

229
00:34:09,115 --> 00:34:14,884
In the yard outside, a few hens have tucked their heads under their wings.

230
00:34:14,884 --> 00:34:23,537
And on a rooftop nearby, a cat sits perfectly still, watching the moon, its tail curled neatly around its paws.

231
00:34:25,237 --> 00:34:32,596
The owl on the temple calls one more time, a soft, falling note that floats out over the rooftops.

232
00:34:32,596 --> 00:34:46,037
And then there is no sound at all, except the leaves, and the far, faint breathing of the sea, and your own slow breath, in, and out, as Athens sleeps around you beneath the moon.

233
00:34:55,737 --> 00:35:06,638
You can rest now, like the family in the courtyard, like the friends who drifted off on their couches, like the councillor nodding by his lamp.

234
00:35:06,638 --> 00:35:14,642
There is nothing you need to carry tonight, no market to open, no loom to finish, no message to wait for.

235
00:35:14,642 --> 00:35:19,673
All of that can wait until morning, and morning is a long way off.

236
00:35:21,373 --> 00:35:25,929
Let your breathing slow, the way the cicadas slowed at dusk.

237
00:35:25,929 --> 00:35:32,838
Let your hands grow heavy, the way the loom weights hang quiet at the end of their threads.

238
00:35:32,838 --> 00:35:44,455
And let your thoughts drift out and away, like the thin thread of incense from the little altar, rising slowly, spreading, and fading into the night air.

239
00:35:46,155 --> 00:35:56,729
High on its rock, the temple stands pale and still under the moon, as it will tomorrow night, and the night after, for a very long time.

240
00:35:56,729 --> 00:35:59,761
The olive tree stirs its silver leaves.

241
00:35:59,761 --> 00:36:02,249
The owls keep their quiet watch.

242
00:36:02,249 --> 00:36:09,323
And below them, the violet-crowned city sleeps, safe and warm, all the way down to the sea.

243
00:36:11,023 --> 00:36:16,539
Thank you for spending this evening in Athens with The Quiet Archive.

244
00:36:16,539 --> 00:36:22,294
The lamps are out, the stories have been told, and the city is dreaming.

245
00:36:22,294 --> 00:36:23,173
Sleep well.

246
00:36:23,173 --> 00:36:23,973
Goodnight.
